! The templates of `frames.dict` against the words they go around, so the
! fixture needs typey-type and phrases as well: the word a frame goes around is
! what another dictionary wrote. `artifacts` names them.
!
! The head writes with the keys, so a frame's stroke is an ordinary match. The
! line after the word is its tail, reported under the same outline.

! ---- templates
!buffer
STPR*T
HAOER
!buffer
STPHO*BT
HAOER
!buffer
SPHO*RPB
HAOER
!buffer
STPHAO*EURT
HAOER

! A brief whose only claim on the stroke was a `-T` fold. A fold is a last
! resort, so the frame comes first: this writes "either here or" and not
! "either here" plus "the".
!buffer
SAO*EURT
HAOER

! The head comes first, so the head takes the capital: ". From here to", not
! ". from Here to".
!buffer
TP-PL
STPR*T
HAOER

! A unit of two words, whose own capitals stay.
!buffer
STPR*T
TPHORB
!buffer
STWAO*EPBD
-FT

! A whole phrase in the hole. The tail follows the unit however many words it
! wrote, because it never reads them.
!buffer
STPR*T
PHO*RPB

! A pair writes punctuation, which has no letter to take the capital, so it
! passes it on and the word keeps it.
!buffer
TP-PL
SKWO*ET
HAOER
!buffer
SPR*EPB
HAOER
!buffer
SPWA*BGT
HAOER

! ---- what a frame shows while it waits
! The close is on screen from the keys, so a writer sees the frame closed and
! the word lands inside it. Nothing has landed here: the case ends with the
! stroke, and the text is the preview.
!buffer
STPR*T
!buffer
SKWO*ET
